WEBVTT

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:08.210
Mock ribbing is a great alternative
for machine knitters to using a ribber,

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.010
hand-manipulating,
or hand-knitting ribbing.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.290
In a previous video, we discussed
options for creating beautiful mock ribs.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.920
But how do you seam them?

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:25.760
With a little planning, you can have
beautiful seams in your mock ribs.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.560
Now, one note, you may have to adjust
the number of stitches you cast on to work

00:00:31.626 --> 00:00:34.370
these tips,
but you can always adjust your stitch

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.240
count after you've
completed your mock ribs.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.720
Let's take a look at one by one mock ribs.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.650
While you're planning your cast on,
be sure that you have a stitch

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.970
on both edges of the knitting.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.360
This will be dedicated
as a seaming stitch.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.290
As you mattress stitch the seam,
just pick up half of each edge stitch.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.680
Done neatly, you'll have a whole stitch
along that seam, and it

00:01:07.746 --> 00:01:09.850
will not be noticeable.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.410
Remember, you'll want to continue seaming
one half of that edge stitch

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.440
beyond the mock ribbing.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.920
For two by one mock ribs,
make sure you have two stitches

00:01:22.986 --> 00:01:25.240
on each end of the knitting.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.040
This time, you'll mattress stitch between
the two edge stitches as you

00:01:30.106 --> 00:01:33.170
normally would with mattress stitch.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:39.090
This will allow you to continue the two
stitch pattern repeat across your seam.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.330
Three by one mock rib takes
a little more planning.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.290
Now, let's say you're using
this for a sock or mitten cuff.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.520
Set up your mock rib with two stitches
on one edge and three

00:01:53.586 --> 00:01:55.370
stitches on the other edge.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.890
When you fold the work to create the cuff,
you'll mattress stitch between the edge

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:07.680
stitches and still maintain
a three-stitch column intact.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:13.570
If seaming mock ribbing has kept you from
using it, please practice these seams.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.480
Mock ribbing is a great technique
for your machine knitting bag of tricks.

